  "strategy": "1. **Approach and Rationale**  \n   **Central Idea**: We use a combinatorial optimization approach based on a **probabilistic mixture of periodic patterns** to construct the step function. This differs from continuous optimization by explicitly decomposing the solution into harmonic components with distinct frequencies, leveraging the insight that optimal solutions often exhibit periodic structure. The mixture allows flexibility to escape local optima while maintaining global coherence.  \n   **Why this approach**: Continuous optimization tends to get stuck in narrow local optima, whereas periodic mixtures enrich the search space. By balancing correlation penalization and mean function sparsity, we target a global minimum of the overlap integral while preserving the step function constraints.  \n   **Bottleneck Addressed**: Previous implementations rely on local gradient steps and periodic patterns alone, which fail to cover diverse solutions. This method explores multi-frequency interactions, improving the trade-off between correlation strength and solution complexity.  \n   **Limitations**: The method assumes periodicity (though not explicitly enforced) and focuses grid optimizations. Continuous domains might further enhance it.  \n\n2. **Mathematical Specification**  \n   - **Variables**:  \n     - \\( h(x) = \\sum_{i=1}^{K} w_i p_i(x) \\), where \\( w_i \\geq 0 \\), \\( \\sum_i w_i = 1 \\) (\\(K\\): mixture components, \\(p_i\\): periodic base functions).  \n     - Base functions \\(p_i(x)\\) are periodic with \\( \\text{period }1 \\) and defined via Fourier components.  \n   - **Objective**: Minimize \\( C_5 = \\max_k \\int_0^{2} h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx \\).  \n   - **Constraints**:  \n     - \\( 0 \\leq h(x) \\leq 1 \\), enforced via projection.  \n     - \\( \\int_0^{2} h(x) dx = 1 \\), achieved by interleaving periodic patterns.  \n     - Normalization: Sum of weights \\( \\sum_i w_i = 1 \\).  \n   - **Optimization Reformulation**:  \n     - Minimize \\( C \\) by penalizing high correlations between \\( h(x) \\) and shifted \\( h(x+k) \\).  \n     - Use an entropy-based prior on mixture weights to ensure diversity (\\( D_{\\text{KL}}(w, \\alpha^{-1}) \\)) and sparsity.  \n   - **Estimators**:  \n     - Correlation at lag \\( k \\): \\( r(k) = \\int_0^{2} h(x)(1-h(x+k)) dx \\).  \n     - Target \\( \\max_k r(k) \\leq C \\) with linear bounds.  \n\n3. **Implementation Procedure**  \n   - **Initialization**:  \n     - Choose \\( n_{\\text{base}} = 3, K=3, \\text{resolution}=64 \\).  \n     - Use periodic sin/cos patterns: \\( p_j(x) = \\cos(2\\pi k_j x) \\) or \\( \\sin(2\\pi k_j x) \\) (\\(k_j\\) integer).  \n     - Initialize \\( w_i \\) uniformly with sum-1.  \n   - **Core Optimization**:  \n     - Update weights with projected gradient ascent (avoid critical points).  \n     - Penalize correlations \\( r(k) > \\alpha \\cdot C \\) via logarithmic barrier.  \n   - **Resolution Adjustment**:  \n     - Linearly interpolate base values to new grid if improvement > 1%.  \n     - Cease if grid \\( \\geq 512 \\) or time > 600s.  \n   - **Fallback**: Return upsampled parent if optimization freezes.  \n   - **Feasibility Fix**: Use clipping-normalization cycle after interpolation.  \n   - **Soln Format**:  \n     - \\( h(x) = \\text{scaled mixture of base patterns} \\).  \n\n4. **Search Choices**  \n   - **Base Templates**:\n     - 5 phases/weights (entropy-constrained).\n   - **Hyperparameters**:  \n     - Tuning: Lag penalty \\( \\alpha \\in [0.5, 1.5] \\), update rate \\( \\beta \\in [0.03, 0.15] \\) (Scheduler: exponential decay if plateau).  \n   - **Diversity**: Mixtures evolve via entropy decoherence.  \n\n5. **Budget & Robustness**  \n   - **Time Estimate**: ~80% spent grid optimizations, 20% on resolution checks.  \n   - **Termination**: Maximum grid \\( n_{\\text{max}}=1024 \\), time per grid \\( \\leq 500\\) s.  \n   - **Solver**: cvxpy for quadratic penalties, mode=`COIN`.  \n   - **Fallback**: Store best candidate across all grids.  \n\n6. **Validation & Interface**  \n   - Feasibility: Check \\( 0 \\leq h[i] \\leq 1 \\) and sum \\( = n_{\\text{points}}/2 \\).  \n   - Score: Re-evaluate \\( C_5 \\) after every 20 grid updates.  \n   - Output: Final \\( (h_{\\text{samples}}, C_5, n_points) \\), with \\( n_points \\) auto-upsampled.",
